{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379639} :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379635}] :question/math-academy-id "q-342425" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x(2x-3){y}^{′}-3y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution ${y}_{1},$ where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3=0.$*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}(x) = x^{1/3}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228686} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}(x) = x^{1/2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228687} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}(x) = x^{-3/2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228688} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}(x) = x^{2/3}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228689} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{1}(x) = x^{3/2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228690}] :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228685}] :question/math-academy-id "q-345653" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$4x^{2}y^{″} - 4xy^{′} + (3 - 4x^{2})y = 0$\nThe general solution near $x = 0$ of the differential equation is given by\n$y = c_{1}y_{1}(x) + c_{2}y_{2}(x)$,\nwhere $c_{1}$ and $c_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution $y_{1}$, where $a_{n}$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, …$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x = 0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $4λ^{2} - 8λ + 3 = 0$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379683 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379685} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-5/6}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379686} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5/6}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379687} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-1/6}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379688} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{1/6}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5/6}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379687} :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379684}] :question/math-academy-id "q-345656" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n36{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+36{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution ${y}_{1},$ where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $36{\\lambda }^{2}-36\\lambda +5=0.$*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{1/4}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379692} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-3/4}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379693} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3/4}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379694} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{4/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379695} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-1/4}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3/4}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379694} :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379691}] :question/math-academy-id "q-345662" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+(8-2{x}^{2})x{y}^{′}-(3+16{x}^{2})y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution ${y}_{1},$ where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $16{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -3=0.$*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379699} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379700} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379701} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-1/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379702} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{1/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379699} :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379698}] :question/math-academy-id "q-345667" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+(6+2{x}^{2})x{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution ${y}_{1},$ where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $9{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -2=0.$*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)=x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402384} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-1}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402385} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402386} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402387} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5/16}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)=x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402384} :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402383}] :question/math-academy-id "q-345668" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-5)x{y}^{′}+(5+2x)y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution ${y}_{1},$ where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $16{\\lambda }^{2}-21\\lambda +5=0.$*"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the First Solution in the General Solution Using the Method of Frobenius" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048197 :topic/math-academy-id 6719 :topic/title "Finding General Solutions Using the Method of Frobenius"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229189 :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228684} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379661} :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379660}] :question/math-academy-id "q-344129" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the missing coefficient in the first solution ${y}_{1}?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE, the indicial equation of the ODE is $3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0,$ and the recurrence relation for the coefficients is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}(\\lambda )=\\frac{4}{(\\lambda +n)(3(\\lambda +n)-2)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe first solution is \n\n${y}_{1}(x)={a}_{0}({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/3}\\,+$ {{field-1}} ${x}^{8/3}+⋯).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228691}] :question/math-academy-id "q-345716" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$xy^{″} + 2(x + 1)y^{′} + 2y = 0$\nThe general solution near $x = 0$ of the differential equation is given by\n$y = c_{1}y_{1}(x) + c_{2}y_{2}(x)$,\nwhere $c_{1}$ and $c_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the missing coefficient in the first solution $y_{1}$?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x = 0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE, the indicial equation of the ODE is $λ^{2} + λ = 0$, and the recurrence relation for the coefficients is\n$a_{n}(λ) = - (2)/((λ + n) + 1)a_{n - 1}$.*\n\nThe first solution is\n$y_{1}(x) = a_{0}(1 - x +$ {{field-1}} $x^{2} + ⋯)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379706} :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379705}] :question/math-academy-id "q-345695" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n2x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-5y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the missing coefficient in the first solution ${y}_{1}?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE, the indicial equation of the ODE is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0,$ and the recurrence relation for the coefficients is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}(\\lambda )=\\frac{5}{(\\lambda +n)(2(\\lambda +n)-1)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe first solution is \n\n${y}_{1}(x)={a}_{0}({x}^{1/2}+\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3/2}\\,+$ {{field-1}} ${x}^{5/2}+⋯).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379709} :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379708}] :question/math-academy-id "q-345701" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the missing coefficient in the first solution ${y}_{1}?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE, the indicial equation of the ODE is $3{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0,$ and the recurrence relation for the coefficients is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}(\\lambda )=\\frac{4}{(\\lambda +n)(3(\\lambda +n)-1)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe first solution is \n\n${y}_{1}(x)={a}_{0}({x}^{1/3}+{x}^{4/3}\\,+$ {{field-1}} ${x}^{7/3}+⋯).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379712} :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379711}] :question/math-academy-id "q-345708" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the missing coefficient in the first solution ${y}_{1}?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE, the indicial equation of the ODE is ${\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0,$ and the recurrence relation for the coefficients is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}(\\lambda )=-\\frac{4}{(\\lambda +n)((\\lambda +n)-3)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe first solution is \n\n${y}_{1}(x)={a}_{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4}\\,+$ {{field-1}} ${x}^{5}+⋯).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379715} :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379714}] :question/math-academy-id "q-345959" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n4x{y}^{″}+2(3x+1){y}^{′}+3y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the missing coefficient in the first solution ${y}_{1}?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE, the indicial equation of the ODE is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0,$ and the recurrence relation for the coefficients is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}(\\lambda )=-\\frac{3}{2(n+\\lambda )}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe first solution is \n\n${y}_{1}(x)={a}_{0}({x}^{1/2}-{x}^{3/2}+\\,$ {{field-1}} ${x}^{5/2}+⋯).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Coefficients of the First Solution Given the Recurrence Relation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/r